{
  "request": {
    "model": "nomic-embed-text:latest",
    "input": [
      "Synthetic nurse note for software testing. No real patient data.\n\nThe nurse reviews a commercial driver's symptoms alongside information from a witness, the treating team and an older workplace form, because each source describes a different part of the story; the occupational record retains those differences and identifies completed current findings without turning a booking, a family account or a recollection into a measured patient result.\n\nThe hospital discharge report confirms that the driver's documented loss of consciousness was caused by hypoglycaemia after investigation, rather than remaining of undetermined nature; the current referral therefore names hypoglycaemia as the diagnosis, but the diabetes treatment history, recurrence risk and return-to-driving specialist advice are not attached, and a glucose value obtained after treatment cannot supply the missing longitudinal assessment.\n\nPart of the usual route requires reversing near workers and reading changing signs before entering a customer site, although today's assessment takes place in a quiet room; the driver is asked to describe the sequence and timing of difficulties, and the note preserves the difference between an occupational concern, a suspected mechanism and a completed clinical assessment.\n\nFor the occupational reviewer, the nurse retains both the definite findings and the limitations of the available account, including the timing and testing conditions where they matter, rather than producing a single reassuring label; the driver is told which information is on file and which details still require confirmation, without replacing the recorded clinical evidence with an employer's preference.\n"
    ],
    "truncate": false
  },
  "provider_called": true,
  "status": "completed",
